<% content_for :title, "After You Fuck Up | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="repair-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="repair-hero">

    <picture class="repair-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_after_you_fuck_up_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_after_you_fuck_up_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="repair-hero-content">

      <p class="repair-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / AFTER YOU FUCK UP
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>WELL, THAT</span>
        <span>HAPPENED.</span>
        <span>NOW WHAT?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="repair-intro">
        GET STEADY. TELL THE TRUTH.<br>
        HANDLE WHAT’S YOURS TO HANDLE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / RIGHT NOW -->
  <section class="repair-section repair-pink">
    <div class="repair-inner">

      <p class="repair-number">01 / RIGHT NOW</p>

      <h2>
        FIRST<br>
        THINGS FIRST.
      </h2>

      <div class="repair-copy">
        <p>
          Before you explain, apologize, or make a promise to change your entire life,
          deal with what needs attention right now. Get somewhere safe. If you’ve been
          drinking or using, don’t drive or make a major decision while impaired.
        </p>

        <p>
          If someone is in immediate danger or needs urgent medical help, get emergency
          help. Otherwise, slow the situation down enough to figure out the next useful
          move. You do not have to solve every consequence tonight.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="repair-grid">

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>WHAT NEEDS ATTENTION NOW?</h3>
          <p>
            Safety? Medical care? A ride home? A bill, missed responsibility, or
            conversation that can’t wait? Separate what is urgent from what feels
            urgent because you’re panicking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>WHO CAN HELP?</h3>
          <p>
            Contact someone steady: a trusted person, your treatment team, or someone
            who can help you make a practical plan. Try: “I fucked up, and I need help
            figuring out the next step.”
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / WHAT HAPPENED -->
  <section class="repair-section repair-black">
    <div class="repair-inner">

      <p class="repair-number">02 / WHAT HAPPENED</p>

      <h2>
        NAME IT.<br>
        WITHOUT THE<br>
        LIFE SENTENCE.
      </h2>

      <div class="repair-copy">
        <p>
          Write down what actually happened. What did you do? What do you know for sure?
          Who was affected? What still needs to be found out? Keep the facts separate
          from your guesses about what everyone thinks of you.
        </p>

        <p>
          “I missed the payment” is something you can address. “I ruin everything” is a
          verdict that gives you nowhere to go. Don’t minimize what happened, and don’t
          turn one action into your entire identity.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="repair-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT HAPPENED — IN PLAIN FACTS, WITHOUT EXCUSES OR SELF-DESTRUCTION?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / SHAME & RESPONSIBILITY -->
  <section class="repair-section repair-yellow">
    <div class="repair-inner">

      <p class="repair-number">03 / SHAME & RESPONSIBILITY</p>

      <h2>
        OWN YOUR SHIT.<br>
        KEEP YOUR<br>
        HUMANITY.
      </h2>

      <div class="repair-copy">
        <p>
          Shame says, “I’m a piece of shit.” Responsibility says, “I did this. It had
          an effect. What do I need to do now?” One keeps you stuck inside a punishment
          loop. The other asks you to act like a fucking adult.
        </p>

        <p>
          Bipolar symptoms, ADHD, addiction, pain, or overwhelm can be part of the
          context. They may help explain what was going on. They don’t automatically
          erase the impact or make repair someone else’s job.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="repair-grid">

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>WHAT SHAME SAYS.</h3>
          <p>
            “I’m hopeless. I always do this. I don’t deserve help. I should feel awful
            forever.”
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>WHAT RESPONSIBILITY ASKS.</h3>
          <p>
            “What did I do? Who was affected? What can I repair? What needs to change
            so I’m less likely to repeat it?”
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / REPAIR -->
  <section class="repair-section repair-purple">
    <div class="repair-inner">

      <p class="repair-number">04 / REPAIR</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT CAN<br>
        YOU REPAIR?
      </h2>

      <div class="repair-copy">
        <p>
          Repair is more than feeling bad. It means acknowledging the impact, taking
          responsibility, and following through on a practical change. You can offer an
          apology, but you can’t demand forgiveness or control how long someone needs.
        </p>

        <p>
          Keep it clear. Don’t make the person you hurt take care of your shame, and
          don’t use your diagnosis or state of mind as a “but” that cancels out what
          happened.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="repair-grid">

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>ACKNOWLEDGE IT.</h3>
          <p>
            Say what you did plainly. Don’t minimize it, blame the other person, or
            make them drag the truth out of you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>MAKE A REPAIR.</h3>
          <p>
            Ask what would be useful, then offer a specific step: replace what was
            damaged, pay what you owe, correct what you said, or keep the commitment
            you made.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>RESPECT THEIR BOUNDARIES.</h3>
          <p>
            They may need distance, time, or no further contact. An apology is an
            offer of accountability, not a request for immediate reassurance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>FOLLOW THROUGH.</h3>
          <p>
            The repair is what you do after the apology. Keep the plan, make the
            payment, attend the appointment, or change the safeguard that failed.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="repair-callout">
        <p>
          “I DID ____. I CAN SEE IT AFFECTED YOU BY ____. I’M SORRY. I’M NOT ASKING YOU
          TO MAKE ME FEEL BETTER OR FORGIVE ME RIGHT NOW. I’M GOING TO ____.”
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / LEARN THE PATTERN -->
  <section class="repair-section repair-black">
    <div class="repair-inner">

      <p class="repair-number">05 / LEARN THE PATTERN</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT LED<br>
        UP TO IT?
      </h2>

      <div class="repair-copy">
        <p>
          Look back to understand the pattern, not to put yourself on trial for six
          hours. What was happening with your mood, sleep, pain, stress, or substance
          use? What was the first choice that moved things in this direction? What did
          you notice and brush off?
        </p>

        <p>
          Pay attention to the familiar bargaining: “Just one,” “I’ll deal with it
          later,” “Fuck it,” or “I can fix this after.” The point isn’t to shame
          yourself for having the thought. It’s to recognize the moment where a
          different safeguard could help.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="repair-grid">

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>WHAT DID YOU NOTICE?</h3>
          <p>
            The urge, the missed reminder, the rising energy, the exhaustion, the
            avoidance, the overcommitment, or the moment you stopped checking in with
            yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="repair-box">
          <h3>WHAT NEEDS TO CHANGE?</h3>
          <p>
            Make the change specific: add a phone reminder, ask someone to check in,
            block an easy route to a risky choice, revise your relapse plan, or tell
            your treatment team what’s been happening.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / THE NEXT STEP -->
  <section class="repair-section repair-pink">
    <div class="repair-inner">

      <p class="repair-number">06 / THE NEXT STEP</p>

      <h2>
        TAKE THE<br>
        NEXT USEFUL<br>
        STEP.
      </h2>

      <div class="repair-copy">
        <p>
          You don’t need a dramatic vow to become a different person by Monday. Pick
          one concrete thing you can do now: make the call, send the honest message,
          put the appointment in your phone, ask for help, or get back to the plan that
          supports your recovery.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can’t undo what happened. You can decide what you do next. Handle one
          consequence at a time, then keep showing up for the changes you said you’d
          make.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="repair-callout">
        <p>
          GIVEN WHAT HAPPENED, WHAT IS THE NEXT RESPONSIBLE THING YOU CAN DO — TODAY?
        </p>
      </div>
<!-- BACK TO TOOLBOX -->
<nav class="index-home-nav" aria-label="Return to toolbox">
  <%= link_to "← BACK TO TOOLBOX",
      toolbox_path,
      class: "index-home-button" %>
</nav>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>